  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Designed and produced under the direction of Andrew Hoyem at Arion Press, San Francisco, with the assistance of Gerald Reddan, Eric Holub, Stephanie Dal Porto, Peggy Gotthold, and Leif Erlandsson. The types are Times New Roman. The intaglio printing was done by R.E. Townsend Studio, Georgetown, Massachusetts, by Robert Townsend and Katherine Hanlon, from plates made by Jenö Gindl, Berlin, and proofed by William Hall at Pace Editions, New York City. The paper is Hahnemühle Copperplate. Published in an edition limited to 75 numbered and 26 lettered copies, each signed by the artist"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An accordian-fold album of 18 photogravures by Jim Dine. The photogravures are preceded by a title page and followed by a colophon and limitation page numbered or lettered and signed by the artist, making an album of 20 printed panels.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Issued in box (44 cm.). The box has inset in the lid, a bas-relief in lead alloy by Dine of the heads of a cat and a monkey, a new work in his "Ape &amp; Cat" series created for this edition.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Includes, in a recess at the bottom of the case: The madonna of the future / by Henry James ; with an introduction by Arthur C. Danto ; and a photogravure by Jim Dine. San Francisco : Arion Press, 1997.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"This edition of The Madonna of the Future is limited to 200 numbered copies for sale and 26 lettered copies hors de commerce. Of the edition for sale, the copies numbered 1 through 75 and lettered A through Z are included with Ape &amp; Cat, an album of 18 photogravures by Jim Dine, in a box with a lead bas-relief on the lid of an "Ape and Cat" image by Dine. This book contains one of the photogravures by Dine from the Ape &amp; Cat series"--Colophon, Madonna of the future.</subfield>
  </datafield>
